course: dictionary
lesson: 12300
title: A richer word for lots
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Copious means in large amounts, more than you need. It is a formal word, so you will meet it in books and school reports more than in everyday chat.
explain: Look at the words around it. «She wrote copious notes during the science talk.» Notes come in plenty here, so copious means a lot of notes, far more than a few.
explain: Try swapping in lots of or plenty to check. «The storm brought copious rain» means the rain was heavy and plentiful. Using copious instead of lots of makes your writing richer.
fact: Copious means in large amounts, more than enough.
fact: «Copious notes» means a lot of notes.
fact: «Copious rain» means heavy, plentiful rain.
fact: «Copious tears» means someone is crying a great deal.
fact: Copious is a slightly formal word, so it sounds richer than «lots of».
exercise: «The baker left copious amounts of cake on the table.» What does copious mean here? | Hidden under a cloth | Burnt and dry | A small slice | *A large amount, more than enough
exercise: Which phrase means the same as «copious notes»? | No notes at all | *Lots of notes | Few notes | Pictures only
exercise: Mia says copious is just a fancy word for lots. Why might a writer choose copious? | It means very small | It means quiet and calm | *It is richer and more formal than lots of | It is a rude word
test: «The river flowed with copious water after the snow melted.» What does copious mean? | Shallow and still | Frozen solid | Dirty and cold | *Plentiful, more than enough
test: Which word is closest in meaning to copious in «copious tears»? | Few | Tiny | *Plenty of | Hidden
exam: «The gardener gave the roses copious water, and they bloomed all summer.» What does copious mean? | *A large amount, more than enough | Cold and dirty | Very little | Only a drop
